1
arXiv:2606.30639v2  [cs.AI]  1 Sep 2026

### 01_from_plan_to_action_how_well_do_agents_follow_th
ASE ’26, October 12–16, 2026, Munich, Germany Shuyang Liu, Saman Dehghan, Jatin Ganhotra, Martin Hirzel, and Reyhaneh Jabbarvand
• RQ1: Standard Plan Compliance (§4.1).To what extent do
agents follow the instructed plan? What factors impact plan
compliance and violations? Does plan compliance help agents
resolve issues?Findings.Agents follow the standard plan, al-
though with varying compliance rates. Some strictly follow the
plan in the specified order, while others adaptively override the
plan based on the trajectory, depending on the problem’s dif-
ficulty. Following the plan positively helps all agents resolve
more GitHub issues. The fine-tuning paradigm, context window
pressure, data contamination, overfitting, and optimization for
short-term reward are the most prevalent factors impacting plan
compliance.
• RQ2: Behavior of Agents in the Absence of Plan (§4.2).How
do agents operate in the absence of a plan? To what extent does re-
moving the plan impact overall performance?Findings.Without
a plan, agents follow their internalized problem-solving strategy,
which overlaps with the standard plan to a varying degree. The
success rate, however, drops in the absence of the standard plan.
• RQ3–RQ5: Impact of Plan Variations (§5.1–5.3).Do agents
heed removal, addition, and reordering of plan phases? To what
extent does frequent reminding of the plan phases help com-
pliance in the long-horizon task of program repair?Findings.
Removing a standard plan phase, even if the agent usually ig-
nores it under the standard plan setting, negatively impacts the
agents’ performance, confirming the overall impact of a global
plan on local reasoning steps. The negative impact of a bad plan
is greater than no plan at all. Surprisingly, augmenting plans with
task-relevant phases inspired by best practices also negatively
affects agents’ performance when they are not aligned with the
model’s internal strategy. Periodic plan reminders reduce plan
violations and improve performance.
• RQ6: Generalization to Other Benchmarks (§6.1).How much
can the observations and conclusions about plan compliance gen-
eralize to another benchmark, i.e., SWE-bench Pro [8]?Findings.
The plan compliance rate of the agents across all settings on SWE-
bench Pro drops by 13%, on average, compared to SWE-bench
Verified. The agents exhibit different phase flow patterns, e.g.,
they give up on generating reproduction tests early and validate
patches using existing regression tests rather than generating
tests. This is likely because SWE-bench Pro instances are more
challenging and less contaminated, and the high-level standard
plan is no longer effective at guiding the agents.
• RQ7: Impact of Nondeterminism (§6.2).To what extent is
plan compliance of agents under different plan settings attributed
to nondeterminism?Findings.Nondeterminism exists but does
not impact our findings. We account for nondeterminism by
repeating experiments and comparing persistent behaviors across
plan settings.
We are thefirstto (1) conduct a large-scale analysis of plan
compliance by agents, (2) introduce novel plan compliance metrics,
(3) speculate the root causes of plan violations, and (4) study how
plan compliance relates to task success. Our findings suggest that
the effectiveness of the plan is tightly coupled to its alignment
with the model’s internalized workflow and the task’s complexity.
Therefore, future research should focus on fine-tuning paradigms
that teach models to follow plans more effectively, rather than
encoding task-specific plans into them.
2 Experimental Design
We aim to analyze whether and to what extent programming agents
follow the specified, task-appropriate software engineering work-
flows. Given the popularity of programming agents for fixing real-
world GitHub issues, this study will focus on program repair. The
default practical workflow for this task involves localizing the bug,
patching the code, and then validating whether the patch resolved
the bug. Many ex

### 01_icm_knowledge_big
A visual workflow map instead of just folders

A file editor in the middle

Claude Code chat on the right

The ability to switch between "I'm driving" and "Claude's driving"

That last one is the key insight. Sometimes I want to edit every individual file. Sometimes I want to automate the whole thing and trust Claude to run the pipeline. A good control surface lets me switch between those modes fluidly.

------

Why Not Just Use Existing Repos?

There are open-source projects that do parts of this. Claude CLI interfaces. Claude Code web UIs. Tools for session persistence and usage tracking.

I looked at them. They're good, but they're not MY workflow. They have features I don't need. They're missing features I want. They don't map to how I actually work.

#### So instead of using them directly, I'm doing this:

Study what they built

Pull the parts that work

Strip away everything I don't need

Build around my existing folder structure

Don't reinvent the wheel. Make a better wheel. Make the wheel yours.

------

The PRD Approach

Before any code gets written, I have Claude create a PRD.

PRD = Product Requirements Document

#### The PRD describes:

What we're building

What existing repos we're pulling from

What stacks those repos use

What I want to keep and what I want to cut

The phases from start to finish

This becomes a markdown file that lives in the workspace. Claude Code reads it at the start of every session. It's persistent context. Stateful prompting.

Why markdown? Because markdown files are small (low token cost), easy for Claude to read, and I can edit them in any text editor. If I want to format them later, I can upload to Google Docs and it converts automatically.

------

#### What the Lessons Cover

1.2: How I Use Claude Code in the Build Process

The initial thinking. David's question that started this. Researching existing repos. Creating the PRD. Setting up the workspace inside my actual project folder so Claude has context.

1.3: Building the Front End (Session 1)

Watching Claude work. Plan mode vs execution mode. Sub-agents that fetch repos and study workspace structure. What happens when Claude makes better decisions than what I told it to do.

1.4: [Coming Soon]

More build sessions as the project continues. This is an ongoing build. New lessons drop as I finish sections.

------

The Build Process Framework

Whether you follow along with my project or build something completely different, the process is the same:

1. Define what you actually     need

Not what sounds cool. What workflow pain are you solving? Can you solve it at Level 1, 2, or 3 instead of Level 4?

1. Research what exists

What repos already do part of this? What can you borrow? What's worth building vs. taking?

1. Map your existing     structure

What folder architecture are you building around? Give Claude your

http://claude.md/

. Let the front-end wrap around what you already have.

1. Write a PRD

### 01_icm_map
#### 3. The ICM Architecture
- **The Five-Layer Context Hierarchy**
    - **Layer 0: Global Identity:** (`CLAUDE.md`) "Where am I?" (~800 tokens).
    - **Layer 1: Task Routing:** (`CONTEXT.md`) "Where do I go?" (~300 tokens).
    - **Layer 2: Stage Contract:** (`stages/XX/CONTEXT.md`) "What do I do?" (200-500 tokens). The core control point.
    - **Layer 3: Reference Material:** (`references/`, `_config/`) "The Factory." Stable rules, style guides. (500-2k tokens).
    - **Layer 4: Working Artifacts:** (`output/`) "The Product." Per-run inputs/outputs. (Variable).
- **Key Distinction:** **Layer 3 (Reference) vs. Layer 4 (Working)**
    - **Layer 3:** Stable, internalize as constraints. (e.g., `voice.md`).
    - **Layer 4:** Dynamic, process as input. (e.g., `research-output.md`).
    - This separation cleans the model's context and improves performance.
- **Stage Contracts (`stages/XX/CONTEXT.md`)**
    - **Inputs Table:** Explicitly lists which Layer 3 and Layer 4 files to load.
    - **Process:** Instructions for the transformation.
    - **Outputs:** Where to write the result (`output/`).
- **Visual Structure & Flow:**
    - Folder numbering encodes sequence (01, 02, 03...).
    - Stage output folders are the handoff points for the next stage or a human reviewer.
    - Creates "Review Gates" for human-in-the-loop editing.

---

#### 4. Evidence & Implementation
- **Workspaces Tested:**
    - Script-to-Animation Pipeline (3 stages).
    - Course Deck Production (5 stages).
    - ICM Workspace-Builder (A workspace that builds workspaces).
- **Early Practitioner Findings:**
    - **U-Shaped Intervention:** Heavy human edits at Stage 1 (direction) and final stage (alignment/debugging). Light edits in the middle.
    - **Accessibility:** Non-developers successfully edit prompts and run pipelines by editing markdown files.
    - **Portability:** Workspaces are duplicated and adapted by copying folders.
- **Threats to Validity:** (Self-reported data, single model family, no formal controlled study).

---

#### 5. Discussion: Scope & Implications
- **Where ICM Works:**
    - Sequential, reviewable, repeatable workflows.
    - Content production, research analysis, policy workflows.
- **Where ICM Does NOT Work:**
    - Real-time multi-agent collaboration (needs message-passing).
    - High-concurrency systems (needs queueing, state isolation).
    - Complex automated branching logic (requires code).
- **Key Implications:**
    - **Observability as a Side Effect:** "Glass-box" workflows due to plain-text artifacts.
    - **Context Scoping:** Architectural prevention of "lost in the middle" vs. post-hoc compression.
    - **Regulatory Alignment:** Natural fit for EU AI Act requirements (human oversight, audit trails).

---

